  L87: Well, the core argument revolves around what Flixian calls field-like constructs. And we see these everywhere, right? In complex systems theory, ecology, economics.
  L155: Flexion actually states this as a very clear foundational slogan. They say, new terminology is not new predictive content.
  L219: Precisely. And to earn what Flixing calls predictive standing, a proposed construct has to pass a strict four-part empirical test.
  L327: So, Flixian argues that if your groundbreaking new metric completely disappears just because you tweaked the coarse graining scale slightly, it's not a fundamental physical property.
  L447: And the reason Flexion chose MIP-E-S as the testing ground is vital, it's because MIP-E-S is already incredibly well understood by existing kinetic and hydrodynamic theories.
  L467: Flexion deliberately chose this because it constitutes a hard test.
  L731: When Flixian ran a validity scan at a high packing fraction, this FMAX metric read exactly 1.000 at time zero.
  L791: But Fliction caught it, they discarded the metric, and they built a composite of relative density domain indicators.
  L927: So Flixian set up a treatment branch and a control branch of the simulation using identically seeded random numbers.
  L1095: In the text, Fliction proudly describes this as a clean failure.
  L1271: Fliction openly admits the pilot study was underpowered.
  L1359: As Flixion says, new terminology is not new predictive content.
  L1423: But if we force these complex algorithms through Flixion's four-part test,
