f they were a bit identical, the intervention had zero effect. It never fired. This is a profound lesson for anyone doing computational causal inference. An inactive intervention, a bug where your code does nothing, and a genuinely null causal effect look exactly the same on a spreadsheet. Both return a divergence of zero. That is terrifying. It means you could write a paper claiming variable X has no causal effect on variable Y and be completely wrong just because your code was broken. The only way to prove your null result is real is to run a positive control check. Yeah. You have to prove that your poke is mechanically capable of doing something. You have to prove the oven can actually turn on. Right. So Flician fixed the relative clock bug, ran a massive perturbation, and confirmed that yes, blasting a region with high speed causes the particles to rapidly evacuate that area and leaves a permanent measurable scar on the global density of the system. The intervention framework was finally validated. The dual universe machine is fueled up. The clock is fixed. The positive control works. It is time for the final showdown, the matched region pilot. The premise here is classic experimental design. If we find two regions in the simulation that had the exact same starting density but very different capacity readings, and we hit them both with the exact same intervention pulse, do they react differently? Does the preexisting capacity predict the magnitude of the response to the poke? If it does, then capacity isn't just a downstream observational metric. It is a real causal driver of the system. So they found four pairs of regions, matched them up carefully on density, ran the interventions in the parallel universes, and measured the response. What were the results? It was explicitly inconclusive. Out of the four pairs, two pairs showed a stronger response in the high-capacity region. The other two pairs showed a stronger response in the low-capacity region. It was a pe